WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.524 --> 00:00:20.929
Hello, and welcome to Superstars in History, the only talk show featuring history's biggest stars.

2
00:00:21.549 --> 00:00:22.629
I'm Faye Moss.

3
00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:30.852
Today's guest was a prolific artist who completed a lot of paintings in a short amount of time.

4
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:37.582
His landscapes, still lifes, and self-portraits are instantly recognizable.

5
00:00:38.763 --> 00:00:43.025
Straight from history, here is Vincent van Gogh!

6
00:00:59.131 --> 00:01:00.572
I was reluctant to come here, Faye.

7
00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:04.170
I don't always like talking to people because I know I'm disagreeable.

8
00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:07.872
Well, I'm glad you're here.

9
00:01:08.313 --> 00:01:10.834
Why don't we— And sometimes I talk too much.

10
00:01:11.455 --> 00:01:13.756
But my brother Theo said I have my good side as well.

11
00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:15.958
He said it's like there are two people in me.

12
00:01:16.598 --> 00:01:19.620
One is selfish and unfeeling, and the other is gifted and gentle.

13
00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:25.004
Speaking of Theo, he played an important role in your life.

14
00:01:25.924 --> 00:01:29.247
Why don't you tell us more about your family and your childhood?

15
00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:33.433
I was born in Zundert, in the Netherlands, in 1853.

16
00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:36.733
My father was a minister, and my family lived comfortably.

17
00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:39.974
Besides Theo, I had another brother and three sisters.

18
00:01:41.095 --> 00:01:44.376
We often took walks in the countryside, and I came to love nature.

19
00:01:45.676 --> 00:01:48.237
But then I went to boarding school where I was quite miserable.

20
00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:51.878
Around age 14, I quit school, ending my education.

21
00:01:52.918 --> 00:01:57.019
As a child did you amaze your family with your artistic ability?

22
00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:00.200
I drew when I was young, but I showed little talent.

23
00:02:01.524 --> 00:02:03.505
My first job was working at an art dealership.

24
00:02:04.086 --> 00:02:05.607
Theo later joined the same firm.

25
00:02:07.888 --> 00:02:10.250
However, I wasn't happy and left after a few years.

26
00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:18.195
I later moved around Europe, working as a teacher, a bookshop worker, and a minister.

27
00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:23.579
But I struggled to find a purpose, which greatly disappointed my parents, I'm afraid.

28
00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:28.462
I finally discovered my true calling when I was 27, with Theo's help.

29
00:02:29.403 --> 00:02:31.064
That sounds dramatic!

30
00:02:31.548 --> 00:02:32.449
What happened?

31
00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:37.613
Theo and I frequently wrote to one another when we were apart, and I often included sketches in

32
00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:38.113
my letters.

33
00:02:39.094 --> 00:02:43.717
Theo urged me to continue drawing, and he even sent me money each month, allowing me to devote

34
00:02:43.757 --> 00:02:44.518
myself to art.

35
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.924
I began taking art lessons in 1880, and I concentrated on learning to draw before beginning to paint.

36
00:02:57.184 --> 00:03:01.847
During this time you painted many scenes of rural life in the Netherlands.

37
00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:08.852
The Potato Eaters is probably your most famous early work.

38
00:03:08.872 --> 00:03:13.155
I always considered it one of my best paintings, though many stupidly disagreed.

39
00:03:14.296 --> 00:03:18.659
I wanted to convey the honest dignity of a family who earned their food through manual labor.

40
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:25.044
I chose dark earth tones and intentionally painted the family rough and coarse, but I found them beautiful.

41
00:03:26.068 --> 00:03:29.029
In 1886 you moved to Paris.

42
00:03:29.869 --> 00:03:32.030
That must have been exciting!

43
00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.450
It was!

44
00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:36.471
Theo was working in Paris, and I joined him there.

45
00:03:37.372 --> 00:03:42.433
The Impressionists, like Monet, were changing the art world with their visible brushstrokes and use of color and

46
00:03:42.473 --> 00:03:49.956
light. I became friends with newer artists like Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec and Georges Seurat.

47
00:03:53.996 --> 00:03:56.057
Oh, and I became interested in Japanese art.

48
00:03:58.218 --> 00:04:00.320
Paris was so lively and inspiring!

49
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.481
But it was also exhausting.

50
00:04:03.561 --> 00:04:06.003
After a few years, I felt old and broken.

51
00:04:07.043 --> 00:04:10.005
So I went to Arles, in the South of France, to recover.

52
00:04:11.085 --> 00:04:12.446
Did your health improve?

53
00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:14.067
Oh, yes.

54
00:04:14.547 --> 00:04:20.010
The sunshine there, along with green and yellow fields as far as the eye could see—nature spoke to

55
00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:23.312
me! I traveled to the coast and painted boats.

56
00:04:24.308 --> 00:04:31.710
I painted peasants working in the wheat fields, orchards in blossom, sunflowers—how I loved sunflowers!

57
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.591
They made me think of happiness and gratitude.

58
00:04:35.471 --> 00:04:39.993
I fully developed my own style in Arles—thick, swirling brushstrokes and vivid colors.

59
00:04:40.953 --> 00:04:44.454
I knew colors faded over time, so I chose strong colors that would last.

60
00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.915
You haven't mentioned your self-portraits.

61
00:04:48.715 --> 00:04:50.256
You painted about 35 of them!

62
00:04:52.396 --> 00:04:54.678
Did you enjoy painting your own face?

63
00:04:55.758 --> 00:04:57.680
I had a practical reason for painting myself.

64
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.342
I never had much money and mainly lived with support from Theo.

65
00:05:02.283 --> 00:05:06.186
Since it was expensive to pay models to pose, I painted myself for practice.

66
00:05:07.226 --> 00:05:08.727
It isn't easy to paint oneself, Faye!

67
00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:16.713
You also planned on starting an artists' colony in Arles, and Paul Gauguin, another artist, visited you there.

68
00:05:17.853 --> 00:05:21.256
I had dreamed of starting a place where artists could live and paint together.

69
00:05:22.036 --> 00:05:25.639
I had met Gauguin in Paris, and I was so thrilled when he joined me in my yellow

70
00:05:25.679 --> 00:05:29.081
house. At first we worked together in harmony.

71
00:05:32.804 --> 00:05:36.086
But then tension developed between us due to our different approaches to art.

72
00:05:36.966 --> 00:05:42.190
Gauguin planned to leave, and I felt hopeless—my dream of an artists' colony was fading.

73
00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:45.232
We had a terrible fight, and I must have become ill.

74
00:05:47.094 --> 00:05:50.316
I woke up in the hospital and was told I had cut off most of my ear.

75
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.861
I have no memory of what happened.

76
00:05:53.401 --> 00:05:56.422
I don't know why you asked me about this Oops.

77
00:05:56.562 --> 00:05:57.462
Sorry, Vincent.

78
00:05:58.042 --> 00:05:59.262
Why don't we move on?

79
00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:02.563
What did you do when you left the hospital?

80
00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:07.625
I was feeling hopeful enough to paint again, but I was worried about my unpredictable health.

81
00:06:08.625 --> 00:06:10.766
So I decided to go to an asylum in Saint-Remy.

82
00:06:11.806 --> 00:06:15.627
I stayed there for a year and finished 150 paintings, sometimes one a day.

83
00:06:16.648 --> 00:06:21.558
I painted still lifes, portraits of other patients, the garden, and the surrounding countryside.

84
00:06:22.378 --> 00:06:27.160
I particularly enjoyed the view from my window, and I even painted the starry night sky from that

85
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.142
window. Are you talking about The Starry Night?

86
00:06:32.203 --> 00:06:34.984
Yes, but I actually painted The Starry Night during the day.

87
00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:38.806
The cypress trees and the village were visible from my window.

88
00:06:39.846 --> 00:06:43.768
The church steeple was my own addition, similar to those from back home in the Netherlands.

89
00:06:45.016 --> 00:06:48.417
Usually I painted from observation, but I painted the stars from memory.

90
00:06:49.257 --> 00:06:53.038
You see, one morning I watched the countryside from my window before sunrise.

91
00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:55.719
The morning star looked very big.

92
00:06:56.639 --> 00:06:59.640
The view was so peaceful and majestic that it felt heartbreaking.

93
00:07:01.401 --> 00:07:07.002
The sight of the stars always made me dream That's so beautiful, Vincent!

94
00:07:08.143 --> 00:07:10.063
Thank you for sharing your art with us.

95
00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:13.164
We'll let you get back to history now.

96
00:07:14.344 --> 00:07:15.545
Yes, very well.

97
00:07:18.667 --> 00:07:24.911
After leaving the asylum, Vincent van Gogh spent his last months in an artists' village near Paris.

98
00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:31.474
Sadly, he took his own life in July 1890 at the age of 37.

99
00:07:32.395 --> 00:07:34.496
Theo died six months later.

100
00:07:35.317 --> 00:07:44.174
Despite producing eight hundred paintings during his short ten-year career, van Gogh received little recognition during his lifetime.

101
00:07:45.295 --> 00:07:53.400
Theo's widow, Johanna van Gogh-Bonger, collected Vincent's paintings and letters and introduced his genius to the world.

102
00:07:54.220 --> 00:08:00.624
Van Gogh's fame grew quickly after his death, and today he is one of the most well-known artists

103
00:08:00.744 --> 00:08:01.785
of all time.

104
00:08:03.006 --> 00:08:06.068
That's all for now on Superstars in History.

105
00:08:07.092 --> 00:08:10.979
I'll be back soon with another fascinating guest from the
